WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:05.863
Grassland experiments give a clear result.

2
00:00:06.803 --> 00:00:10.563
Plots with more plant species generally grow more aboveground biomass.

3
00:00:11.643 --> 00:00:16.283
Losing species can therefore change what an ecosystem does: how much plant material

4
00:00:16.283 --> 00:00:21.543
it produces. Richer communities also tend to have steadier total production from

5
00:00:21.543 --> 00:00:26.883
year to year. At Cedar Creek in Minnesota, the total became steadier even though

6
00:00:26.883 --> 00:00:28.943
individual species became less stable.

7
00:00:29.743 --> 00:00:33.823
The important result is about the whole community, and how its different species

8
00:00:33.823 --> 00:00:34.823
work together.

9
00:00:35.633 --> 00:00:37.253
Why can a mixture produce more?

10
00:00:38.193 --> 00:00:42.353
Complementarity means species, on average, do better together than their

11
00:00:42.353 --> 00:00:43.433
monocultures predict.

12
00:00:44.313 --> 00:00:47.253
They may use resources differently, or help one another.

13
00:00:48.333 --> 00:00:50.453
Legumes adding nitrogen are one example.

14
00:00:51.633 --> 00:00:53.033
Selection is another explanation.

15
00:00:53.953 --> 00:00:58.693
A richer random draw is more likely to include a highly productive species that

16
00:00:58.693 --> 00:01:01.213
dominates. Both can act together.

17
00:01:01.713 --> 00:01:06.553
The Loreau and Hector partition separates complementarity and selection in the

18
00:01:06.553 --> 00:01:11.573
yields. But the partition alone does not identify the biological mechanism.

19
00:01:12.717 --> 00:01:14.317
Now ask what steadier means.

20
00:01:15.277 --> 00:01:20.297
Temporal stability is mean total biomass across years divided by its standard

21
00:01:20.297 --> 00:01:26.057
deviation. If species fluctuate independently, their ups and downs partly cancel in

22
00:01:26.057 --> 00:01:30.037
the total. A higher mean production can also raise stability.

23
00:01:31.057 --> 00:01:34.657
Resistance means how little production changes during a disturbance.

24
00:01:35.697 --> 00:01:38.777
Resilience means how fully or quickly it returns afterwards.

25
00:01:39.657 --> 00:01:45.417
Grassland experiments found greater resistance to wet and dry climate events, but no

26
00:01:45.417 --> 00:01:48.737
detectable diversity effect on recovery one year later.

27
00:01:49.750 --> 00:01:51.390
How do we know richness matters?

28
00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:57.570
At Cedar Creek, researchers sowed randomly chosen species with the same seed mass in

29
00:01:57.570 --> 00:02:01.270
every plot. They clipped, dried and weighed the plants each year.

30
00:02:02.390 --> 00:02:07.030
Random composition prevents researchers from simply choosing productive species for

31
00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:12.970
richer plots. BIODEPTH repeated the logic across 8 European grassland sites.

32
00:02:13.850 --> 00:02:18.170
Across those sites, losing plant species reduced average aboveground biomass.

33
00:02:19.333 --> 00:02:21.493
Real landscapes add a complication.

34
00:02:22.593 --> 00:02:26.653
Wetter or more fertile soil could support both more species and more biomass.

35
00:02:27.633 --> 00:02:33.473
Field studies found positive biodiversity and biomass associations after adjusting

36
00:02:33.473 --> 00:02:35.033
for environmental differences.

37
00:02:35.853 --> 00:02:38.473
But an unmeasured variable could still drive both.

38
00:02:39.373 --> 00:02:40.633
Experiments assign richness.

39
00:02:41.693 --> 00:02:43.093
Surveys need that extra caution.

40
00:02:44.117 --> 00:02:46.657
One smaller point: the benchmark matters.

41
00:02:47.537 --> 00:02:51.037
A mixture can beat the average monoculture without beating the best.

42
00:02:51.937 --> 00:02:56.357
Extra yield against the average does not mean it outproduces every species grown

43
00:02:56.357 --> 00:02:56.697
alone.
